WEBVTT

00:00:00.029 --> 00:00:03.559
In Core, Inventory separates the item catalogue from what

00:00:03.559 --> 00:00:05.239
the department currently holds.

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:10.420
On hand provides Items and Location filters, while Unit and Person lookups

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:12.449
help identify a particular holder.

00:00:12.909 --> 00:00:17.049
Start by choosing the right item and location before interpreting a quantity.

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:21.309
This walkthrough follows one fictional training consumable through recorded

00:00:21.309 --> 00:00:24.590
software movements, rather than claiming that anyone physically

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.739
inspected or transported supplies.

00:00:26.939 --> 00:00:31.280
The Items tab lists catalogue entries and their Tracking mode and Status.

00:00:31.709 --> 00:00:33.910
New item opens the catalogue workflow.

00:00:34.459 --> 00:00:39.239
On How it is tracked, this example uses By quantity with Track lots

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:41.270
and Expiration required selected.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:44.379
Controlled substance and Kit item remain off.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:48.009
Decide how an item should be tracked before stock exists;

00:00:48.379 --> 00:00:50.070
later changes can be restricted.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.170
Industrial teams can apply the same distinction to bulk consumables

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.019
and individually tracked equipment.

00:00:56.189 --> 00:01:00.080
The Review page brings the prepared item together: Academy training

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:03.560
batteries, measured in packs, with its code and description.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:09.439
It also shows minimum stock five, reorder point eight, and the fictional unit cost.

00:01:09.819 --> 00:01:11.779
Read these values before Save.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:16.280
A catalogue entry describes the item; it does not itself receive twenty

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.510
packs or prove a physical purchase.

00:01:18.879 --> 00:01:23.399
The next screens use that item in an existing saved lot and stock movement.

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:26.799
Lots identifies batches separately from quantities.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.639
The saved ACADEMY lot A row belongs to Academy training batteries and

00:01:31.639 --> 00:01:33.919
displays a November thirtieth expiry.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:37.910
New lot is available for creating another batch, but this view

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:39.469
demonstrates the saved row.

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:43.560
Check the lot and expiry when recording a movement so that later stock

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.800
history refers to the intended batch.

00:01:46.159 --> 00:01:48.800
Creating a lot alone does not add stock.

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:52.960
In Adjust stock, the prepared Review shows Receive, the training

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.270
item and lot, Station as the destination, and Amount twenty.

00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:00.829
Its note clearly identifies a software exercise.

00:02:01.249 --> 00:02:04.619
Read the complete movement and choose Record movement only when

00:02:04.619 --> 00:02:06.259
it matches the intended receipt.

00:02:06.769 --> 00:02:10.359
The filtered On hand result then shows twenty packs at Station.

00:02:10.849 --> 00:02:14.679
That stored balance is the result of the receipt, rather than proof supplied

00:02:14.679 --> 00:02:16.390
by the preparation screen alone.

00:02:16.589 --> 00:02:21.779
New transfer reviews the same item and lot, with Station as Source location, Unit

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.780
as Destination location, and Amount five.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.789
Confirm both ends before Record movement.

00:02:28.219 --> 00:02:32.879
A generic location label does not identify a particular apparatus by itself.

00:02:33.359 --> 00:02:36.910
The Transfers list then shows the saved Station-to-Unit move.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:41.570
Search and rescue or wildland crews can use this pattern for a documented

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:45.609
restock, while keeping software movement distinct from actual delivery.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:49.870
History shows the recorded Receive of twenty and Transfer of five

00:02:49.919 --> 00:02:51.120
for the training batteries.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:55.850
The transfer appears as one visible row with Station pointing to Unit,

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:57.930
and its note explains the purpose.

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:01.669
Use the item and location filters to understand a change.

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:05.960
Attest as witness and Rebuild stock balances are separate controls;

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:10.010
this example reads the ledger without invoking either operation.

00:03:10.219 --> 00:03:13.350
Issue equipment reviews two packs from Station for Matthew

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:17.040
Casey, together with the expected return date and a training note.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.269
Confirm the person and quantity before Issue.

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:25.790
The Issuances result shows Personnel, two outstanding, and a Return action.

00:03:26.219 --> 00:03:30.889
The named recipient was established in the review; this table uses the holder label.

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:35.059
Community response and security teams can use that distinction to track

00:03:35.059 --> 00:03:36.819
supplies signed out to a person.

00:03:36.989 --> 00:03:41.070
A later saved Issuances view shows Partially returned and

00:03:41.159 --> 00:03:42.869
one pack still outstanding.

00:03:43.249 --> 00:03:47.420
This is a partial return, not a completed return of everything issued.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:51.249
The Return action remains available for the outstanding balance.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:54.679
Here we review that stored result; the intervening

00:03:54.679 --> 00:03:56.730
return-entry dialog is not shown.

00:03:57.169 --> 00:04:01.150
Check the remaining quantity and status instead of treating any return as

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:03.169
evidence that the whole issue is closed.

00:04:03.389 --> 00:04:07.760
At this point in the example, the balance table shows fourteen packs at Station,

00:04:07.999 --> 00:04:10.370
five at Unit, and one at Personnel.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:15.809
Those twenty packs reconcile the receipt, transfer, issue and partial return.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:20.150
These are the quantities in this dated snapshot, before later usage.

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:24.019
In your own department, read the current filtered balances and exact

00:04:24.019 --> 00:04:27.589
holder context rather than copying the demonstration quantities.

00:04:27.709 --> 00:04:31.209
Counts, alerts and reports contains Inventory alerts.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:34.949
This captured row is a Low stock alert for the training batteries,

00:04:35.149 --> 00:04:37.700
marked Resolved across All locations.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:42.299
The page also describes expiry and overdue-return alerts, but no

00:04:42.299 --> 00:04:44.249
expiring-lot result is shown here.

00:04:44.729 --> 00:04:47.319
Refresh alerts is a separate available action.

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:52.229
Read the alert type and status alongside stock data; a resolved alert does

00:04:52.229 --> 00:04:54.069
not replace a current stock review.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:59.439
Inventory reports opens Choose a report with Inventory on hand displayed.

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:04.900
Continue leads toward Narrow it down, but this view is the chooser, not

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:07.450
a generated report or exported PDF.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:12.529
Fire and EMS administrators, like other services, should verify a report’s

00:05:12.529 --> 00:05:14.889
scope before relying on its numbers.

00:05:15.309 --> 00:05:20.180
Keep catalogue setup, recorded movements, stock counts and reporting distinct

00:05:20.379 --> 00:05:24.520
so that another person can understand what each result actually establishes.

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:29.120
The same process supports fire and EMS, community paramedicine and

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:32.789
mental health response, search and rescue, security departments and

00:05:32.789 --> 00:05:37.659
companies, wildland contractors, and industrial emergency response teams.
